## Logging

Logging is initialised automatically when the package is imported.  Log files
are written to `logs/` at the project root.

### Basic usage

```python
from {{ pkg_name }}.logging import get_logger

logger = get_logger(__name__)
logger.info("Application started")
logger.debug("Value: %s", some_value)
logger.warning("Something looks off")
logger.error("An error occurred", exc_info=True)
```

### Configuration

Set these keys in your `.env` file:

| Key | Default | Description |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `LOG_LEVEL` | `INFO` | Minimum level to record (`DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARNING`, `ERROR`) |
| `LOG_RETENTION_DAYS` | `30` | Log files older than this many days are deleted on startup |
| `LOG_CONSOLE` | `true` | Set to `false` to suppress terminal output |

Log files are named `MMDDYYYY.log` and rotated daily by filename.

### Rich terminal output

When `LOG_CONSOLE=true`, output is formatted with [Rich](https://rich.readthedocs.io)
(coloured levels, pretty tracebacks, timestamps).

For output that is *not* part of the logging system (progress bars, tables,
user-facing messages), use `rich.console.Console` directly:

```python
from rich.console import Console

console = Console()
console.print("[green]✓[/green] Done")
console.print("[bold red]Error:[/bold red] something went wrong")
```

Rich markup uses `[colour]text[/colour]` tags.  See the
[Rich documentation](https://rich.readthedocs.io/en/stable/markup.html) for the
full list of styles.
